0,Neo-Aramaic dialect of Hertevin - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neo-Aramaic_dialect_of_Hertevin,"Hertevin is a dialect of Northeastern Neo-Aramaic originally spoken by Chaldean Catholics in a cluster of villages in Siirt Province in southeastern Turkey. Speakers of Hértevin Aramaic have emigrated mostly to the West, and are now scattered and isolated from one another.",Hértevin,Afro-Asiatic
1,Afroasiatic languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afroasiatic_languages,"There are many well-attested Afroasiatic languages from antiquity that have since died or gone extinct, including Egyptian and the Semitic languages Akkadian, Biblical Hebrew, Phoenician, Amorite, and Ugaritic.",Hértevin,Afro-Asiatic
2,Hertevince - Vikipedi,https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hertevince,"1970 yılında dil bilimi uzmanı Otto Jastrow tarafından keşfedildi. 1999 yılındaki nüfusları yaklaşık 1.000 kişidir.[2] Konuşanları iki dilli olup ikinci dil daha çok Kürtçedir.[2]. SIL International kuruluşu olan Ethnologue'a göre 3 lehçesi bulunur: Hértevin Proper (Arton), Umraya, Jinet.[2].",Hértevin,Afro-Asiatic
3,"Hértevin Language (HRT) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map ...",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/hrt/,"Hértevin is an endangered indigenous language of Turkey. It belongs to the Afro-Asiatic language family. Direct evidence is lacking, but the language is thought to be used as a first language by the elderly only. It is not known to be taught in schools.",Hértevin,Afro-Asiatic
